[{:db/id 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-31312c0a-f5f5-5c45-80ec-b73eead72363"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9675b0c7-9ed4-51ad-88df-5786551b6bd9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354389 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354402 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-31312c0a-f5f5-5c45-80ec-b73eead72363" :question/id #uuid "31312c0a-f5f5-5c45-80ec-b73eead72363" :question/math-academy-id "q-83975" :question/problem "![](images/37/37a6eb13f8a6f6ab46040495894dedeec4951b4814aad19f45bfbd4ed7040767.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/89/897028515a29fdc4174d2a101439a81a30895db41ac3d29f311d53e0e6a79e29.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3248d29-8c2f-5b6e-bca3-612d18b52286"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3248d29-8c2f-5b6e-bca3-612d18b52286" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3248d29-8c2f-5b6e-bca3-612d18b52286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-709e5a2e-54c5-5f5c-b473-eb287ff2a6d1" "answer-8e198036-a474-5058-bfdb-866d7d95cca0" "answer-bd29b008-2b41-5c5a-b870-fc78e9661939" "answer-d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" "answer-df3f0225-7ec2-5288-9ed6-196d002eb9e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-709e5a2e-54c5-5f5c-b473-eb287ff2a6d1" :answer/id #uuid "709e5a2e-54c5-5f5c-b473-eb287ff2a6d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e198036-a474-5058-bfdb-866d7d95cca0" :answer/id #uuid "8e198036-a474-5058-bfdb-866d7d95cca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd29b008-2b41-5c5a-b870-fc78e9661939" :answer/id #uuid "bd29b008-2b41-5c5a-b870-fc78e9661939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" :answer/id #uuid "d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df3f0225-7ec2-5288-9ed6-196d002eb9e7" :answer/id #uuid "df3f0225-7ec2-5288-9ed6-196d002eb9e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354391 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/ca/ca09f228fc218ef1b57196c6b6b1e740c10173b7cd38b1c27407726eccd783f1.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354405 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (4-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-12\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-0] \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9675b0c7-9ed4-51ad-88df-5786551b6bd9" :question/id #uuid "9675b0c7-9ed4-51ad-88df-5786551b6bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-83998" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fca2d0c37c8831a66c3c183b31b84b41fd5a16740ff13fd86f4877bdaeb60a6.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/10/1060c071df1dbd197c14da529819a34bd4457868fdd4cbe78a7f93f12dbf67da.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (2-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd7d38f6-a7fa-5865-98e5-ea6506d22a4c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd7d38f6-a7fa-5865-98e5-ea6506d22a4c" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7d38f6-a7fa-5865-98e5-ea6506d22a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-52b423e4-8572-5efa-b98b-b676b2c178b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52b423e4-8572-5efa-b98b-b676b2c178b6" "answer-9ce35c76-148f-5bb3-a99f-541b58a26f70" "answer-8c54d999-f74e-5c26-b531-09a8860a231c" "answer-a0af3adb-9d86-553c-acba-e8f816cbacfd" "answer-be87cc15-09cf-5525-b687-f5d34bc230e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52b423e4-8572-5efa-b98b-b676b2c178b6" :answer/id #uuid "52b423e4-8572-5efa-b98b-b676b2c178b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ce35c76-148f-5bb3-a99f-541b58a26f70" :answer/id #uuid "9ce35c76-148f-5bb3-a99f-541b58a26f70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c54d999-f74e-5c26-b531-09a8860a231c" :answer/id #uuid "8c54d999-f74e-5c26-b531-09a8860a231c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{{e}^{4}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0af3adb-9d86-553c-acba-e8f816cbacfd" :answer/id #uuid "a0af3adb-9d86-553c-acba-e8f816cbacfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be87cc15-09cf-5525-b687-f5d34bc230e6" :answer/id #uuid "be87cc15-09cf-5525-b687-f5d34bc230e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}]
